ate. - PTH Levels: Elevated PTH. - BMD: Low BMD. - Calcitriol: Decreased (due to impaired kidney function).."}, "syn_report": "Question: A 55-year-old man with a history of chronic glomerulonephritis due to IgA nephropathy presents to your office with bone pain. Which of the following laboratory findings would you most expect upon analysis of this patient's serum? \nOptions: {'A': 'Increased PTH, decreased calcium, increased phosphate, decreased calcitriol', 'B': 'Decreased PTH, increased calcium, increased phosphate, increased calcitriol', 'C': 'Increased PTH, increased calcium, decreased phosphate, decreased calcitriol', 'D': 'Decreased PTH, decreased calcium, increased phosphate, decreased calcitriol', 'E': 'Normal PTH, normal calcium, normal phosphate, normal calcitriol'} \nTotal Analysis: **\nIn a 55-year-old man with chronic glomerulonephritis due to IgA nephropathy presenting with bone pain, the most expected laboratory findings would be elevated calcium and phosphate levels, elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels, and low bone mineral density (BMD). Additionally, calcitriol levels would be decreased due to impaired kidney function. These findings collectively indicate secondary hyperparathyroidism and the associated bone-related complications in CKD. \n", "vote_history": [{"Nephrology": "yes", "Rheumatology": "yes", "Endocrinology": "yes", "Hematology": "yes", "Bone Disease": "yes"}], "revision_history": [], "syn_repo_history": ["Question: A 55-year-old man with a history of chronic glomerulonephritis due to IgA nephropathy presents to your office with bone pain. Which of the following laboratory findings would you most expect upon analysis of this patient's serum? \nOptions: {'A': 'Increased PTH, decreased calcium, increased phosphate, decreased calcitriol', 'B': 'Decreased PTH, increased calcium, increased phosphate, increased calcitriol', 'C': 'Increased PTH, increased calcium, decreased phosphate, decreased calcitriol', 'D': 'Decreased PTH, decreased 